Thanks for joining us today.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.873
We're going to be talking
about "Mission Possible,

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:11.908
Moving Your Most-Sensitive
Data to GCP."

00:00:11.908 --> 00:00:13.200
My name is Christopher Johnson.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.850
I typically go by CJ.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.650
I'm a security product manager.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.352
I look after compliance,
regulated data.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.060
And what we're talking
to you today about

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.100
are shared workloads
for government.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.057
I have with me Suzanne.

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:26.140
SUZANNE KAO: Hi, everyone.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.340
Great to see everyone.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.140
I'm Suzanne Kao.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.540
I'm a managing
director at Deloitte.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.780
I oversee our global
export controls practice

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.380
and focusing on
regulations related

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.290
to export controls,
as well as sanctions,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.620
and working with CJ today on
this fantastic presentation,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.865
so looking forward to it.

00:00:44.865 --> 00:00:47.490
CHRISTOPHER JOHNSON: All right,
well, at the very base of this,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.532
we want to talk about the
challenge of compliance

00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:50.360
in cloud.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.860
Compliance, what we've learned
over the last couple of years

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.770
is, a lot of the regulations,
a lot of the compliance

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.020
regimes that we look
after weren't written

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.150
with cloud computing in mind.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.410
And a lot of them
reflect requirements

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.410
that come from previous
world of having everything

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.910
in a single data center
or in a single room.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.210
And our customers require
something a little bit more

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.620
complicated than that.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.530
However, as we move to
this, managing compliance

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.625
with multiple data security
regulations in a cloud

00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:21.000
environment can
be really complex

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.158
and slow, especially
for government agencies.

00:01:23.158 --> 00:01:25.200
And this is what we've
really been thinking about

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.200
in trying to imagine
solutions for this.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.890
It gets extra
complicated when you

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.510
have workloads
that have to comply

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.940
with multiple different
compliance and export

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.245
regulations.

00:01:35.245 --> 00:01:36.870
I'll let Suzanne talk
a little bit more

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.700
about the cloud
compliance landscape.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.020
SUZANNE KAO: Thanks, CJ.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.360
So talking about the cloud
compliance landscape, when

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.480
it comes to sensitive data
and moving to the cloud

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.370
environment, there are
a lot of regulations

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.750
that you have to consider.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.260
Amongst them is export controls,
as well as cybersecurity.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.170
And what those
really regulate are

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.430
who can have access, what
can be uploaded to the cloud,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.890
and then how can it be accessed.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.010
So thinking about
that, export controls

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:09.120
can be quite stringent when
it comes to sensitive data

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.500
or controlled data.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.780
And so as you can
see, it restricts

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.120
the unauthorized transfer
of technology and data

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.560
to non-US persons from an
export controls perspective.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.310
So there are a lot of
different mechanisms

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.650
that a company can
employ to make sure

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.440
that it's in compliance
with these regulations.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.850
And one of them is--

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.940
one of the leading practices is
marking that data as controlled

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.292
or not controlled.

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:33.750
And because of the fact that
export control regulations are

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.790
so stringent, a lot
of other regulations

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.660
can piggyback off the
various different compliance

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.310
requirements.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.890
So for instance, when it comes
to cybersecurity regulations,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.190
which govern the confidential
unclassified information,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.160
and in other instances,
classified information,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.980
being able to employ a
program where you're already

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.870
marking the data
can also capture

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.302
some of the cybersecurity
requirements as well.

00:02:58.302 --> 00:02:59.760
But certainly,
there are some times

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.760
that there are conflicts
with these regulations.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.650
So for instance, in certain
jurisdictions around the world,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.660
there is a lot
around data privacy

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.793
and with respect to nationality.

00:03:08.793 --> 00:03:11.460
So if you're thinking about from
an export controls perspective,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.610
where you need to identify
who the non-US persons are,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.990
there are
considerations that you

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.350
have to resolve when
it comes to sharing

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.760
that kind of
personal information

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.693
in order to be in compliance
with the export controls.

00:03:23.693 --> 00:03:25.110
And there are
different mechanisms

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.620
that you need to think about
when you're trying to determine

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.660
who, what, and how.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.200
And some of those are through
performing such things

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.770
as screening, where you identify
certain parties that you can

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.580
or cannot do business with,
licensing, which really means,

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.250
is about, should
you need to have--

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.740
provide access to a non-US
person, from that perspective,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.250
there are licensing mechanisms
that you can actually

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.920
ask the government for
permission to do so.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.730
And then, also
visitor management--

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.080
so in the instances that you
have a physical facility where

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.410
you're needing to
look at, whether it's

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:04.860
the physical parameters of the
building, or the server rooms,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.010
or whatever it might be, there
are other protocols around that

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.530
to control who and who
doesn't have access

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:14.010
to those types of
physical locations.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.990
And that relates also
to access control.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.464
And that is from a
virtual perspective

00:04:18.464 --> 00:04:19.839
such as in the
cloud environment,

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:24.060
where it's technology, or
it's a physical access.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.970
And then with respect
to disclosures,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.810
just like in
cybersecurity, if there

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.870
are instances of
non-compliance, there

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.340
are ways in which
you can actually

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.740
disclose those types of
incidents to the government

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.635
and to resolve those
sort of situations.

00:04:38.635 --> 00:04:40.260
But as you can see,
it's quite complex.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.600
And there are a lot of
different considerations

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.960
that come into play when
it comes to compliance

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.860
and moving to a
cloud environment.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.573
However, should you have the
right protocols in place,

00:04:50.573 --> 00:04:52.740
and of course, the right
tools in place to make sure

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.590
that you do monitor the who,
how, and what of the data,

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.580
and the access of that data,
and the controls of that data,

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.232
then it creates a smooth
transition for such a platform.

00:05:04.232 --> 00:05:06.690
CHRISTOPHER JOHNSON: Migrating
government data to the cloud

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:07.758
is challenging.

00:05:07.758 --> 00:05:09.300
And there is a few
key questions that

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.350
must be answered
before you get started.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:14.370
And really, cloud users need to
be confident of their answers

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.200
before they can
start the migration.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.250
And pretty soon, we'll
get to the solutions

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.000
to these problems.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.958
But for now, let's
understand a little bit more

00:05:20.958 --> 00:05:21.923
about what it takes.

00:05:21.923 --> 00:05:23.340
You have to really
understand what

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.160
information will be
stored or accessed

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.020
in the cloud environment.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.790
As Suzanne mentioned,
that goes back

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.135
to one of the fundamental
pieces of this,

00:05:32.135 --> 00:05:33.510
is understanding
your information

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.590
and tagging it appropriately.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:39.690
The next is where will that
information ultimately reside.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.730
That could be
on-prem in a server.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.470
That could be in the
cloud environment.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.262
But it also is really
important in terms

00:05:45.262 --> 00:05:47.595
of choosing which regions,
which cloud regions your data

00:05:47.595 --> 00:05:49.080
is stored in.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.000
We'll talk here in a few
more minutes about that.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.480
And then we also
need to understand

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.790
what the process is to
grant and manage access

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:57.690
to that information.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.645
What are the controls
that are in place?

00:05:59.645 --> 00:06:01.020
Some of those,
you get to inherit

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.212
from the cloud provider.

00:06:02.212 --> 00:06:04.170
And some of those, you
need to manage yourself,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.190
in terms of looking
after the data,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.318
making sure you have the
right controls in place.

00:06:08.318 --> 00:06:10.860
That, of course, feeds directly
into the next question, which

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.210
is, who can access that data?

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.487
So we put this great
access control around it.

00:06:14.487 --> 00:06:16.320
Now you need to make
sure you're controlling

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.770
the people that have access to
the information appropriately.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.750
Do they have the right
attributes to access

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:22.922
the data in the first place?

00:06:22.922 --> 00:06:24.630
Do they have the right
background checks?

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.480
These are really
important considerations

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:31.380
that feed into the question
of who can access that data.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.240
The next question
to answer here is,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.230
what is the process to add
content to the application

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:37.770
system or platform?

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.402
So we've spun up
our environment.

00:06:39.402 --> 00:06:41.610
We've put all these awesome
access controls in place.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.040
We have the right people.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.415
Now we need to
understand what it

00:06:44.415 --> 00:06:47.060
takes to move data in and
potentially out of the platform

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.770
or out of the
environment once you have

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.250
things configured and working.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.890
The next one is what the
encryption protocols are

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:54.970
and how keys are managed.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.667
This one's actually pretty
exciting, and one of the areas

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.500
that we really try to
help our customers out

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.210
with in Google Cloud.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:03.230
And if you're talking
about US government data,

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.760
it has to be FIPS 140-2
validated cryptography.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.215
And it's really important
that you have that in place

00:07:09.215 --> 00:07:11.590
and you have good controls
around the cryptography that's

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:13.990
used, but you also have
to have a lot of options

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:16.770
in terms of managing
the encryption keys.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.000
And in the Google
Cloud environment,

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.070
you have a lot of choices
with customer managed

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.450
encryption capabilities,
and all of those, of course,

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:30.260
being FIPS 140-2 validated
and turned on by default.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.740
And then finally, how and
where are the data backups

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:33.670
being stored?

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.890
Where is this information
being stored and processed?

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.744
This is also a
key consideration.

00:07:37.744 --> 00:07:41.140
And some of it is baked into
the environment directly.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:43.090
But in other cases,
you need to make sure

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:44.832
that you're managing
this yourself.

00:07:44.832 --> 00:07:47.290
SUZANNE KAO: And I just wanted
to add to some of the points

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.320
that CJ raised.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.778
And I think these are really
critical questions when you're

00:07:50.778 --> 00:07:53.530
thinking about migrating to a
government-- government data

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:57.010
to a cloud platform, is that
you can see that it really

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.020
is about multiple
responsibilities

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.090
and multiple
responsibility owners,

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.850
where thinking about
what information is going

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.265
to be stored, where the
information is going to be,

00:08:06.265 --> 00:08:08.140
how is it going to be
accessed, and so forth.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:11.700
And so even thinking about
our relationship, Deloitte's

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.550
relationship with Google
and working with CJ's team,

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:17.080
that's really been
a collaboration.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:19.270
And being able to identify
and having those answers

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:22.870
very clearly laid out for these
various different questions,

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:26.470
that will inevitably create
that pathway and that journey

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.512
to being able to do that
migration very smoothly.

00:08:28.512 --> 00:08:29.928
CHRISTOPHER JOHNSON:
So now we get

00:08:29.928 --> 00:08:32.799
to make the transition to
my favorite part here, which

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:35.169
is we spent a lot of time
talking about the complexity

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.840
and challenges and the things
that Google and Deloitte are

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.330
doing to make this
easier and try

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:42.700
to help our customers
through the migration

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.670
and bringing their most valuable
information to the cloud

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:46.503
provider.

00:08:46.503 --> 00:08:48.670
So we have a new product
called the Shared Workloads

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:49.760
for Government.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.260
And this is our
solution to the problem.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.760
The idea is, you're going to
secure your sensitive workloads

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.430
and accelerate your path to
running compliant workloads

00:08:57.430 --> 00:08:58.135
on Google Cloud.

00:08:58.135 --> 00:09:00.550
And we're going to talk a lot
more about what that means

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:03.090
and how we're going about that.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.370
So Shared Workloads,
what is this product?

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.530
Basically, this product
allows you to confidently

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.290
secure and configure
those sensitive workloads

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.080
in alignment with the government
requirements and regulations

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:14.800
in just a few clicks.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.420
You get to choose your
compliance security settings.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.170
And we're going to put
the necessary cloud

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.130
controls in place.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.700
It doesn't mean
you're off the hook.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:24.030
You do have shared
responsibility here.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.670
As both Suzanne and I
mentioned, it's really important

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.980
to make sure that that data
is managed and controlled

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.450
correctly.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.510
But on our side, on the Google
Cloud side, we're making it--

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.420
we're streamlining that process
to configure the environment,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:37.950
make sure it's set
up appropriately

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.500
for those compliance outcomes,
and getting you started

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.970
on the path towards compliance.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.440
How does this work?

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.320
Well, for starters,
you log in and start

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.845
using a Shared Workloads.

00:09:47.845 --> 00:09:49.470
This is available
from the Google Cloud

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.180
console, which we'll show
you here in a few minutes.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:54.390
And you access the Shared
Workloads interface.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.420
This is optimized for customers
in regulated industries.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.140
And it's as simple as using
this New Workload button.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.090
We're using this
workload concept

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.740
to put this
information together.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.240
You set up your new workload.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.570
This is where you configure
your platform controls

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:12.300
for data location,
administrative access,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.530
and encryption.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.828
Again, back to what we
were talking about earlier,

00:10:15.828 --> 00:10:17.370
these are those
fundamental questions

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:19.740
you have to answer before
you start moving government

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.152
data to Google Cloud.

00:10:21.152 --> 00:10:23.110
You have to understand
where your data is going

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:26.230
to be stored and processed, who
can access that information,

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.230
and make sure that the
appropriate encryption is

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:29.770
in place.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.110
And then finally, monitor
your project details.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.320
So make sure that
the environment

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.670
is set up appropriately and
that you're keeping track

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:37.920
of that environment over time.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.537
And we help you with the
Shared Workloads product.

00:10:41.537 --> 00:10:43.870
So we'll talk through a little
bit of the use case here.

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.620
I'll let Suzanne
describe the use case.

